Starlink, a leading global broadband provider built on a Low-Earth-Orbit (LEO) satellite network, is rapidly expanding dedicated mobility plans for land, maritime, and aviation users. While several recent works have studied various aspects of the Starlink network, the policies governing how Starlink assigns Points of Presence (PoPs) and orbital satellites to mobile users remain largely unknown. In this paper, we present the first country-scale view of Starlink's PoP and satellite assignment strategy to mobile users in the US. Through a 6,100 km cross-country driving campaign from Boston to Los Angeles in August 2025 covering 7 Starlink PoPs, we curate a multifaceted dataset spanning TCP throughput, latency, user terminal telemetry, DNS-based PoP identification, and satellite ephemeris. We report three key findings. First, PoP-to-user assignment in the mainland US follows a Voronoi tessellation over available PoP locations, with principled adjustments for administrative boundaries, regional IXP capacity, content availability, and maritime support. Second, PoP-level performance shows substantial regional diversity, with median TCP downlink throughput varying nearly twofold across PoPs. Third, Starlink V2 and V1 satellite generations are uniformly assigned to our terminal, and marginal throughput and latency differences across the dominant generation-inclination combinations (V2@43° and V1@53°) indicate that per-beam load rather than satellite capability governs user-perceived performance.